    compare(coupling): the panes follow only where the two extents meet, and a sheet that stays wide says why

    The 7-20 bbox fix made the overview set genuinely global, and the harness
    immediately showed what the coupling did with a world sheet next to a Zürich
    sheet: pane 1 (1546 “[Weltkarte]”, −180…180 / −90…90) zoomed twice lands on the
    tropics, pane 2 (“Das Zürychgow”, 8.05…9.17° E) stayed on its whole sheet with no
    word about it — `extentToView` clamps the box to the sheet, and a box that far
    outside collapses to the whole sheet, so the pane looked untouched.

    - `iiif.js`: `intersectExtents(a, b)` (the shared area, `null` when the boxes do
      not meet) and `coupledView(box, bounds, extent)` → `{ view, coverage }` with
      `full` / `partial` / `outside`. The mapped region is now the **intersection**,
      never the clamped box: a box reaching beyond the sheet must not be squeezed
      onto the sheet edges, which would show the wrong place.
    - `compare.js`: `propagate()` maps the shared part per pane, marks the pane
      (`data-coverage`) and says what happened — “Ausschnitt von Karte 1 auf Karte 2
      übertragen (gezeigt wird der gemeinsame Bereich)”, “… liegt ausserhalb der
      belegten Ausdehnung von Karte 2 – dort bleibt das ganze Blatt”. The pane itself
      carries the sentence as a cantonal warning note (4 px bordeaux bar, `role=status`)
      instead of leaving the visitor to guess why one map stayed wide.
    - Unit tests: the intersection of overlapping/edge-touching/invalid boxes and all
      three `coupledView` outcomes, including the measured world/Zürich pair.

    Measured: `npm run check` ✓ 174/174 tests (was 172), `wrangler deploy --dry-run` ✓.

